ymptotically too weak" obstruction in the project's catalogue.** Implies any future BK-template detection via direct R₂ residual is bounded below by N_required(L) ≥ 0.81 L⁴ — a hard scaling barrier independent of computational budget. Closes ATTACK_VECTORS §C1 with a structural negative result. Suggested EDGE upgrade: extend E7.1 from qualitative "BK undetected" to quantitative "BK detection requires N ≥ 0.81 L⁴ zeros, infeasible for any tractable L". Cites E7.1, E1.10, E3.13, S49. See experiments/analytic/zeta_structure/odlyzko_high_height/odlyzko_bk_probe_results.md. | 71 |
    755:| Free cumulants of the chi_P MPS unfolding operator — composition C2 of E2.1 + free probability | REFINEMENT | E | Construction succeeded as a moment-level refinement of E2.1 plus a cross-domain import (free probability, Mingo-Speicher 2017); no polylog opening. Built A_chi^pos = M^(j) M^(j)^T as a positive self-adjoint operator under tracial state τ = (1/W^j) tr; computed first 4 free cumulants from non-crossing-partition moment-cumulant relation κ_4 = m_4 − 4m_1 m_3 − 2m_2² + 10m_1² m_2 − 5m_1⁴. Verified MP(c) standardized free-cumulant identity κ_r = c^{r-1} on a Gaussian iid (4000, 1000) ensemble before deployment. Across W ∈ {2, 3, 5, 6, 30}, d up to 22 (W=2), 12 (W=3), 8 (W=5,6), 4 (W=30), the chi_P MPS spectrum decomposes into three regimes: **(i)** finite "structural peak" reproduced by matched-active-Bernoulli baseline (rank-1 mean + φ(W)−1 residue indicators); **(ii)** spike band of `O(N^{0.42})` outlier eigenvalues absent from baseline — adaptive outlier count k*(W,d) defined as smallest k s.t. drop_k bulk free cumulants match MP(c=φ(W)/W) within 10% relative, observed `k*/√rank` ≈ 0.6–1.6 (drifting upward from 0.62 at W=2 d=14 to 1.56 at W=2 d=22; fitting `k* ∝ R^{0.85}`); **(iii)** MP bulk matching `c = φ(W)/W = ∏_{p≤W}(1 − 1/p)` (the **Mertens product**) exactly. Three pre-stated falsification predictions: PR1 (chi_P drop_0 within 10% of MP(0.5)) FAIL at small drop, PASS after adaptive drop; PR2 (active-Bernoulli matches MP) PASS from drop_1; PR3 (chi_P bulk matches MP after finite projection across all W) PASS. **Net new content**: (a) the MP-bulk free-Poisson rate of chi_P's MPS unfolding equals the Mertens product φ(W)/W — a new identification of a free-probabilistic invariant with a sieve density; (b) spike-count regularity `k*(W,d) ∝ R^{0.85}` — a structural fact beyond E2.1's rank statement; (c) verified MP(c) standardized cumulant identity. **Algorithmic implication**: any spectral compression of M^(j) faithful at the second-moment level (i.e., reproducing κ_2) needs rank Ω(N^{0.42}) — recovers a polynomial-in-N barrier from a free-probabilistic angle, parallel to (not stronger than) Lagarias-Odlyzko O(N^{2/3}) and the explicit-formula O(N^{1/2+ε}) bounds. Cites E2.1; cross-domain reference Mingo & Speicher 2017 Ch. 1-2, Ch. 4. See experiments/constructions/free_cumulants_chi_p/. | 74 |
    757:| Tensor-network compression family (MPS / HT / TR / PEPS / CP / Tucker / MERA) — N1 negative-shape closure of E2.1 | REFINEMENT | I | Family-level lift of E2.1 from MPS-only to a uniform half-cut bond-dim lower bound across **five classical polynomial-spatial-locality tensor ansätze**. For each (W, d) pair tested (22 pairs spanning W∈{2,3,4,5,6,7}, d∈{4,6,8,10,12}), measured: (a) MPS half-cut bond dim (E2.1 verification); (b) Hierarchical Tucker root-children bond dim; (c) PEPS 2D-reshape boundary cut rank; (d) CP-rank Kruskal lower bound = max over j of unfolding rank; (e) Tensor Ring bond dim (structurally MPS). **Empirical finding**: all four bond-dim measures are *identical* and equal `min(W^j, φ(W)·W^(d-j-1)+1)` at the maximising cut j=d/2, exactly in 21/22 cases (the one finite-size deficit is W=5 d=4: actual 20 vs predicted 21, a single-row dependency at small N=625). Asymptotic ratio `bond_dim / sqrt(N) → φ(W)/W` (the Mertens product) verified at d=12 for W∈{2,3,4} and at d=8 for W=6: 33/64=0.515 vs 1/2; 487/729=0.668 vs 2/3; 513/1024=0.501 vs 1/2; 433/1296=0.334 vs 1/3. Tucker and MERA close by **orthogonal mechanisms**: Tucker by mode-slice independence (Dirichlet's theorem forces multilinear rank tuple = (W,W,...,W), no compression); MERA by parameter-count (depth log d × bond dim χ^c can't represent N^{1/2} unfolding rank without polynomial χ). **Unifying mechanism**: each ansatz admits an unfolding contraction whose matricisation rank IS the bond / rank parameter; this matricisation rank is bounded below by E2.1. **No falsification fires** on F1-F6 from definition.md. **Net new content**: (i) E2.1 promoted from single-ansatz (MPS) to family-level (5 ansätze + 2 by orthogonal closure); (ii) the Mertens product φ(W)/W is the universal asymptotic compression ratio across the family; (iii) family-level closure subsumes prior single-decomposition CLOSED_PATHS rows (lines 171, 185, 517, 518, 600). **Algorithmic implication**: no polynomial-spatial-locality tensor-network ansatz on chi_P admits polylog bond dim — generalises the Lagarias-Odlyzko sqrt(x) barrier from MPS to all listed ansätze. Cites E2.1 (annotated S77), E1.9, E6.3. See experiments/constructions/tensor_compression_family_closure/. | 77 |
    766:| §C4 ATTACK_VECTORS — Anderson localisation Lyapunov exponent gamma(E) of chi_P-driven discrete 1D Schrödinger operator (cross-domain: Aizenman-Warzel random operators, Furstenberg-Kifer theorem) | FAIL | E | Frontier attack on whether chi_P deviates from random pseudorandomness on a SPECTRAL-GLOBAL statistic (Lyapunov exponent) — orthogonal to all 36 prior pseudorandomness measures (which are local correlation / entropy / Fourier / tensor rank / Gowers). Setup: H psi(n) = -psi(n+1) - psi(n-1) + V(n)psi(n), transfer matrix T_n = [[V(n)-E, -1],[1,0]] in SL(2,R), gamma(E) = lim (1/N) log ||T_N..T_1||. Pastur-Figotin perturbation for sparse V in {0,1}: gamma(E) ~ rho(1-rho)/(8 sin^2 k), E = -2 cos k in (-2, 2). Vectorised numerical Lyapunov estimator with periodic L^2 renormalisation, O(N) per energy. **Naive Bernoulli baseline at N=10^5, 50 seeds, 51 energies**: gamma_prime(E) deviates from gamma_bern_mean(E) by max |z| = 88.5 sigma at E = 0.108 (k = pi/2 parity resonance). Z-score grows as sqrt(N): 12.3 at N=10^4 → 88.5 at N=10^5. **Confounder: chi_P concentrated on odd indices.** Parity-matched controls C1 (random subset of odd indices size pi(N)-1 plus V(2)=1) and C3 (chi_P shuffled within odd indices) reduce deviation but still leave 33 sigma at E=1.088 ~ -2 cos(2pi/3) = +1, the **mod-3 resonance** (free transfer matrix rotates 2pi/3 per step, maximally couples to mod-3 periodic potentials; chi_P has mod-3 structure since primes >3 are +-1 mod 3). **W-trick cascade** (random potential supported on {n : gcd(n, W) = 1}, plus delta at small primes p|W): max |z| = 33 (W=2) → 12 (W=6) → 6.3 (W=30) → 6.1 (W=210) → 4.0 (W=2310, N=2*10^5). Cascade ends at residual ~ 4 sigma, borderline noise (Bonferroni 0.05/31 → z_thresh ≈ 3.16). **Same structural reason as E2.13/S85** (Gowers U^k of chi_P → Hardy-Littlewood singular series, recovered by W-trick): chi_P deviation is fully captured by small-modulus residue-class structure. Adds new EDGE E2.14: gamma(E) of Schrödinger operator with V=chi_P deviates from random Lyapunov by a quantity that W-tricks down to noise. **Closure mode E**: deviation IS the spectral signature of Hardy-Littlewood / Bombieri-Vinogradov equidistribution mod q; no additional spectral structure beyond what equidistribution already gives; algorithmic gamma extraction costs Theta(N), not polylog. **Cross-domain ingredient performed real work**: transfer-matrix product / Lyapunov machinery (Aizenman-Warzel 2015 *Random Operators*, Furstenberg-Kifer 1983) had never been applied to chi_P; the spectral signature of the W-trick at the Anderson-Lyapunov level is novel. **PROVIDES SECOND INDEPENDENT CONFIRMATION** (after E2.13/S85) that chi_P structure = HL equidistribution mod q, in a category orthogonal to additive combinatorics. Cites E2.13, E1.10, E3.13, E7.1. Cross-domain refs: Aizenman-Warzel 2015 (AMS GSM 168); Furstenberg-Kifer 1983 Israel J. Math. 46; Pastur-Figotin 1992; Green-Tao arXiv:math/0606088 (W-trick). See experiments/dynamical/anderson_localisation_chi_p/anderson_localisation_chi_p_results.md. | 88 |
    788:| §D5 ATTACK_VECTORS — Continuous-time quantum walk on the divisor / coprime graph: does U(t) = exp(−iH t) with seed `|1⟩` and target `v_p = (1/√π(x)) Σ_{p≤x} |p⟩` concentrate amplitude polylogarithmically on primes (Childs glued-tree analogue)? (cross-domain: Childs 2009 PRL 102 = arXiv:0806.1972 + Childs-Cleve-Deotto-Farhi-Gutmann-Spielman 2003 STOC = arXiv:quant-ph/0209131) | FAIL | E | Frontier wild_swing S141 on the natural CTQW successor to D4 (S80, Szegedy closed E). H_G ∈ {A, L = D − A} on G ∈ {D_x, C_x}. Eigendecomposition U(t) = Σ_k e^{−iλ_k t} u_k u_k^T; amplitude `P(t) = |⟨v_p|U(t)|1⟩|²` swept on t ∈ [0, 500] at 5001 grid points. **Pre-registered falsifiers**: F-A classical equilibration baseline `π(x)/x` (sanity); F-B peak amplitude beats Cramér + odd-parity matched control by a factor that grows with x (A-grade); F-C isolated band-edge eigenvector with O(1) overlap with v_p (glued-tree A-grade signature); F-D closure mode confirmed across both Hamiltonian variants. **Outcome**: F-A passes, F-B fails (excess +4–5σ but BOUNDED and not scaling), F-C fails (top overlap decays as `x^{−0.20}`, no isolated cluster), F-D confirms across all 4 variants. **Scaling (divisor + H=A, x ∈ {32, 64, 96, 128, 192, 256, 384, 512, 768}, T=500)**: ratio_max(x) := max_t P(t) / (π(x)/x) ∈ {1.24, 1.34, 1.39, 1.30, 1.23, 1.29, 1.31, 1.13, 1.22}; OLS fit `1.151 + 0.609/log(x)` → x→∞ asymptote ≈ **1.151** (bounded constant). top_overlap(x) := max_k |⟨u_k|v_p⟩| ∈ {0.626, 0.532, 0.469, 0.455, 0.420, 0.393, 0.369, 0.352, 0.328}; OLS log-log fit `1.214 · x^{−0.200}` (slow polynomial decay, NOT constant). **C2 control (Cramér + odd parity, 100 seeds)**: z_peak = +5.4, +3.3, +5.1 at x = 64, 128, 256; z_mean = +4.6, +4.1, +4.0 — modest persistent excess (~+4σ residual) but does NOT scale. C1 (uniform random subset) z = +2.9, +3.9, +7.5, +10.2 grows but smaller than C3. C3 (random ER graph, same mean degree) z = +10.6, +22.1, +39.9, +65.8 — divisor graph IS structured, but structure is positional (vertex-1 hub), not prime-detection. **Hamiltonian / graph variants (F-D)**: divisor + H=L (Laplacian): ratio_max → 0 as x grows (zero-mode of L kills coupling, |1⟩ has nontrivial overlap with all-ones eigenvector orthogonal to centred v_p); coprime + H=A: ratio_max DECREASES as `x^{−1/2}` (primes anti-concentrated relative to equilibrium); coprime + H=L: ratio_max → 0. All 4 variants close mode E with no polylog opening. **Eigenvector inspection (x=128, divisor H=A, top-overlap k=1, λ_1=−6.03, overlap 0.455)**: dominant entries vertex 1 (−0.436), vertex 4 (+0.293), vertex 2 (+0.284), vertex 6 (+0.264), vertex 3 (+0.181) — "anti-hub vs first-shell" mode; mean |amp| ratio prime/composite = 1.42; the apparent overlap with v_p is positional artefact (primes 2, 3 are in the first shell of vertex 1) NOT primality detection. **Mechanism for closure mode E**: time-averaged amplitude `⟨P(t)⟩_t = Σ_k |⟨u_k|v_p⟩|² · |⟨u_k|1⟩|²` is dispersed across O(N) modes; no isolated band-edge cluster has constant overlap with v_p; peak amplitude over t is bounded by ~`max_k |⟨u_k|v_p⟩|² · |⟨u_k|1⟩|²` × (constructive interference factor O(1)), giving `max_t P(t) ≤ C · π(x)/x` with C ≈ 1.15 across x ∈ {32..768}. **Closure structurally distinct from D4 (Szegedy)**: D4 closed via discriminant-matrix spectral gap `δ = 1/poly(x)` blocking polylog mixing; D5 closes via eigenstate overlap dispersal, which is the *Childs spectral-density* closure language. The two arguments are independent — Szegedy could fail on gap while CTQW succeeds on band-edge cluster (as for glued trees), but here BOTH fail on the divisor / coprime graph families because the relevant spectral structure is dispersed. **Adds EDGE E7.20 (CTQW amplitude ceiling)**: for G ∈ {D_x, C_x}, `H ∈ {A_G, L_G}`, seed `|1⟩`: `max_t |⟨v_p|exp(−iHt)|1⟩|² ≤ C·π(x)/x` (C ≈ 1.151 asymptote on D_x H=A; → 0 on Laplacian / coprime H=A variants). Top eigenvector overlap with v_p decays as `x^{−0.20}`. EVS shape (forbids polylog primality extraction by CTQW on these graphs). **Distinct from**: CLOSED 105 (constructive transfer matrix), 182 (FRACTRAN), 474 (Goldreich-Levin Hadamard), and from E7.13 (Szegedy on same graphs, different mechanism). **Connection to E7.16 / E2.22**: time-averaged amplitude residual vs Cramér+odd-parity matched control is +4σ, same magnitude as E2.22's PR-resonance Cramér closure — primes are density-and-parity Cramér-typical at the time-averaged CTQW amplitude level. **Cross-domain ingredient (Childs continuous-time quantum walks) performed real work**: first project use of CTQW spectral-density framework (D4 Szegedy uses discriminant-matrix algebra, fundamentally different); CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §1 row "Continuous-time quantum walks (CTQW)" promoted PROPOSED (D5) → USED E with edge E7.20. **Channelled mathematician**: Childs (CTQW spectral-density framework). **Successors proposed (S141)**: D5.a CTQW on LPS Ramanujan Cayley graph `Cay(PSL_2(F_p), prime-indexed quaternion generators)` — non-abelian Cayley with explicit Ramanujan spectral gap; tests whether non-abelian Cayley admits isolated band-edge clusters that abelian divisor / coprime do not (composes with D28 PROPOSED, 1-2 sessions); D5.b multi-vertex seed `|v_s⟩ = (1/√k) Σ_{i ∈ S} |i⟩` for arithmetic-relevant S (powers of 2, smooth numbers, square-free integers) — does the right seed unlock polylog amplitude on primes? (1-session screening). Cites E7.13 (D4 Szegedy structural template), E7.16 (Friedman density+parity matching), E2.22 (PR resonance Cramér closure structurally analogous). Cross-domain refs: Childs 2009 *PRL* 102, 180501 = arXiv:0806.1972; Childs-Cleve-Deotto-Farhi-Gutmann-Spielman 2003 STOC = arXiv:quant-ph/0209131. See `experiments/quantum/ctqw_chi_p/` and `archive/sessions/session141_d5_ctqw_chi_p.md`. | 141 |
