    773:| C3 (NOVELTY_CHALLENGES §1) — Brandt obstructions × per-bit difficulty: per-bit family `{π_J : J = 0..N-1}` under bounded-Kt | REFINEMENT | E | Composition of E5.8 (Brandt-class barrier on π(x) mod 2 — `MKtP not in DTIME[O(n)]` from ePrint 2024/687, S51 closure) + E1.3 (per-bit difficulty profile of π(x), S28 — Fourier weight / influence / R-correlation cleanly stratify bits at boundary J ≈ 0.5N). Defined π_J(x) := bit J of π(x), s_J^(N) := (π_J(0), ..., π_J(2^N - 1)) ∈ {0,1}^(2^N), and the per-bit family F_N = {s_J^(N) : J = 0..N-1}. Imported brandt_mktp.py's bounded-Kt simulator (3-bit-per-op stack VM, ops {PUSH 0, PUSH 1, DUP, FLIP, RPT2, RPT4, ALT, HALT}, L_MAX = 12, T_MAX = 4096, INF = 61). Computed Kt_b(s_J^(N)) for N ∈ {3..7}, J ∈ [0, N-1]. **Pre-stated falsifiers (3 outcomes)**: F1 (uniform saturation → DUPLICATE-PLUS), F2 (J-stratified at E1.3's 0.5N cut), F3 (anomalous). **Result: F2 holds in the meaningful saturation regime N ≥ 4** (N=3 truth-tables are 8 bits long, below VM's saturation threshold and consistent but uninformative). Crucial sharpening: the bounded-Kt cut location is at **J*(N) := ⌈log₂(π(2^N - 1) + 1)⌉ ≈ N − log₂ N**, NOT at E1.3's 0.5N. For J ≥ J*(N), π_J ≡ 0 on [0, 2^N) and Kt_b ∈ {5..8} (program: RPT4 → 4-zeros-per-step). For J < J*(N) — including J's in E1.3's "easy zone" J ∈ [⌈0.5N⌉, J*) — Kt_b = INF = 61 (saturated). Concrete examples: N=5 J=3 (13/32 ones, R-correlated) saturates; N=6 J=3 (34/64 ones, R-correlated) saturates; N=7 J=4 (75/128 ones) saturates. Numerical cuts: N=4: J*=3 vs 0.5N=2 (gap +1); N=5: J*=4 vs 2.5 (gap +1.5); N=6: J*=5 vs 3 (gap +2); N=7: J*=5 vs 3.5 (gap +1.5). The bounded VM is **blind to E1.3's smooth/oscillatory transition** at the Fourier cut — it only resolves the *trivially-zero* boundary; bits in E1.3's "easy zone" but below J* saturate exactly like hard-zone bits. **Structural extension argument** (independent of empirical data): the four Brandt obstructions O1-O4 still apply to every fixed π_J in the per-bit family. (O1) Each π_J is a fixed total Boolean function; per-bit decomposition is parameter-controlled but each π_J is not oracle-dependent. (O2) π_J answers "is bit J of π(x) set", not "is z complicated"; no self-referential Kt inequality. (O3) The per-bit family supplies J*(N) ≈ N − log₂ N strings per N, but they are FIXED, not traversal-path-dependent fresh prefixes — Brandt's Lemma 2 needs an infinite supply of growing-length Kt-random prefixes generated on demand by oracle access, not a finite stack of pre-determined strings. (O4) Uniform DTIME bound, not circuit. The "minimal weakening of O1 admitting Brandt's TRAVERSE" the C3 spec asked about does NOT exist on {π_J : J = 0..N-1}. **Closure mode E** (equivalence): per-bit decomposition is structurally orthogonal to Brandt's diagonalisation skeleton; C3 reduces to E5.8 wholesale at the structural level. **Empirical novelty** (the only new content beyond E5.8 + E1.3): bounded-Kt cut location J*(N) ≈ N − log₂ N is a quantitative sharpening of E1.3's Fourier cut, expressing the limit of what stack-VM-class programs can resolve about per-bit difficulty. Detecting the smooth/oscillatory transition at the Kt level would require a richer VM with arithmetic primitives (LOG2, LI_APPROX, DIV_LOG, GEO_SUM — the R^{-1} kernel) — proposed as successor C3.a. **Cross-domain ingredient**: Brandt 2024 ePrint 2024/687 bounded-Kt simulator (3-bit stack VM), reused from S51. **Adds new finite-N empirical fact** (not previously measured): per-bit Kt-bounded cut at J*(N) ≈ N − log₂ N, gap to E1.3's 0.5N cut grows as 0.5N − log₂ N. Cites E5.8, E1.3, E1.9, E5.3. See experiments/constructions/brandt_per_bit/. | 105 |
    789:| C3.a (NOVELTY_CHALLENGES §1, S105 successor) — Arithmetic-primitive bounded-Kt VM × per-bit difficulty: does adding LOG2 / LI_APPROX / DIV_LOG / GEO_SUM to the bounded-Kt simulator shift the cut on `s_J^(N) := (bit_J(π(0)),...,bit_J(π(2^N-1)))` from S105's `J*(N) ≈ N − log₂ N` toward E1.3's `⌈N/2⌉`? | REFINEMENT | E | Composition of E5.8 (Brandt-class barrier — structural argument inherited unchanged) + E1.3 (per-bit difficulty profile — refined inline). Built 4-bit-per-op extended VM (16 ops: 8 base {PUSH0/1/N, INC, ADD, SUB, MUL, SHR1, DUP, EMIT_S, EMIT0, HALT} + 4 R^{-1}-kernel arithmetic primitives {LOG2, LI_APPROX, DIV_LOG, GEO_SUM}) with integer stack alongside bit-emit stream; T_MAX = 4096, INT_CAP = 10⁹. C inner loop (`sim.c → sim.so`) batch-evaluates one program against all targets simultaneously; pre-skip of programs with no EMIT in pc cycle saves ~50% of enumeration. **Pre-stated falsifiers**: F1 (full shift to ⌈N/2⌉ — every easy-J compresses, every hard-J saturates); F2 (no shift — easy zone all saturated as in original VM, DUPLICATE-PLUS); F3 (intermediate hierarchy — some easy J compress, others saturate). **Result: F3 holds globally (intermediate hierarchy)**, with structure: per-N at L_max=24 (matched to target_lens 16/32/64), the cut moves fully to ⌈N/2⌉ for N ∈ {4, 5} and reverts to J*(N) for N=6; at L_max=28, N=6 partially shifts (J=4 compresses, J=3 still saturates). **Within-easy-zone J-monotone hierarchy identified**: bits closer to J*(N) (the trivial-zero boundary) compress at smaller L_max; bits closer to ⌈N/2⌉ (the E1.3 hard-easy boundary) require larger L_max — the bounded-Kt cost of bit-J of π is empirically monotone in N − J. **Headline compressing programs (L_max=24)**: (N=4, J=2) Kt_b'=33 prog `ADD, LI, LI, EMIT_S, PUSH_N, PUSH_N` — emits `bit_0(LI(LI(2x)))` which EXACTLY matches `bit_2(π(x))` on [0, 16); (N=5, J=3) Kt_b'=33 prog `EMIT_S, LI, DUP, INC, PUSH_N, PUSH0`. Triple-LI program `EMIT_S, PUSH_N, LI, LI, LI, DUP, EMIT_S` (L=28) compresses (N=6, J=4). **Falsified**: original C3 spec's S105 prediction "if YES, R^{-1}-kernel-equipped VM resolves the smooth/oscillatory transition" was correct AT SMALL N (4, 5) but breaks at N=6; the predicted full shift is L_max-budget limited. **Negative content**: hard-zone (J < ⌈N/2⌉) cells remain saturated at L_max ≤ target_len for every tested N ≥ 4; no compressing programs found within budget for hard bits. The structural orthogonality of Brandt's TRAVERSE to the per-bit family (all four obstructions O1-O4 of E5.8) is unaffected by VM choice — empirical compressibility of some `s_J^(N)` does not enable a Brandt diagonalisation. **Quantitative regime caveat**: at L_max > target_len the hard-easy distinction begins to fray (e.g. L_max=28 spuriously "compresses" N=4 hard zone J=0,1 because 28-bit programs can express any 16-bit pattern combinatorially); the meaningful regime is L_max ≤ target_len. **Refines E1.3 inline** with VM-richness hierarchy: 3-bit stack VM cuts at J*(N), 4-bit arith VM cuts at ⌈N/2⌉ for N ≤ 5 with within-easy-zone monotone J-ranking. Cross-domain ingredient: arithmetic-augmented bounded-Kt simulator (analogous to Levin Kt with non-trivial primitive set) — first project use of Li-kernel primitives inside Kt simulator. **Adds new finite-N empirical fact** (not previously measured): bounded-Kt cut location is VM-richness-AND-N-dependent with explicit hierarchy. Cites E5.8 (unchanged), E1.3 (refined inline), E1.5, E1.9 (LSB still saturates), E5.3. Successors proposed (S150): C3.a.i L_max=32 sweep (resolves N=6, J=3); C3.a.ii larger N at random-program sampling; C3.a.iii VM-budget vs RH-scale linkage to S146 F1 results; C3.a.iv ablation drop/swap of arithmetic primitives. See `experiments/constructions/brandt_per_bit_arith_vm/`, `archive/sessions/session150_c3a_arith_vm.md`. | 150 |
